      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The law",
      "heading": "What the law says",
      "html": "<p>Regulation 36 of the Gas Safety (Installation and Use) Regulations 1998 puts a duty on landlords of rented homes. They must arrange a gas safety check every year by a Gas Safe registered engineer. The result goes on a gas safety record.</p><p>The HSE enforces it. A breach can mean a fine, an improvement notice or prosecution. You complete the record. The landlord keeps it and gives it to tenants. Each has their own duty.</p><p>It covers rented homes. It does not cover homes lived in by their owner.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Required fields",
      "heading": "The required fields under Regulation 36",
      "html": "<p>Regulation 36(3) sets the minimum. A CP12 missing any of these is not compliant.</p><ul><li>The date of the check. This is the day you did it, not the day you printed it.</li><li>The full address of the property checked.</li><li>The name and address of the landlord or their agent.</li><li>Each appliance or flue: make, model, type and place. List each one.</li><li>Any defects. Write \"nil\" if there are none. A blank is not the same.</li><li>Any action taken, such as turning off and labelling an appliance ID (Immediately Dangerous).</li><li>That each appliance is safe to use.</li><li>Your own Gas Safe number, not the firm's.</li><li>Your full name, as on the Gas Safe Register.</li><li>Your signature.</li><li>The next check date, 12 months on.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
