    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Obligations",
      "heading": "What landlords must do with the record",
      "html": "<p>Getting the CP12 is not the end of the duty. For a new tenancy, the tenant must get the latest record before they move in. For an existing tenancy, the landlord must give the new record within 28 days of the check. The tenant does not have to ask.</p><p>Keep each record for at least 2 years. Most solicitors say keep it for good. A landlord who cannot find the record is in a weak position.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Enforcement",
      "heading": "When a CP12 is missing or incomplete",
      "html": "<p>A missing CP12 is a breach, not a paperwork slip. The HSE can serve an improvement notice. It can also prosecute, with unlimited fines. A CP12 with fields missing may not count either. One with no Gas Safe number does not. One that skips the gas fire does not.</p><p>The knock-on is wide. After a gas incident, an insurer may refuse to pay. A buy-to-let lender may call in the loan.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Unsafe appliances",
      "heading": "When an appliance fails",
      "html": "<p>When you find an unsafe appliance, the record matters more. The gas industry unsafe situations procedure has three classes. Immediately Dangerous (ID), At Risk (AR) and Not to Current Standards (NCS). An ID appliance must be disconnected before you leave. An AR appliance gets a warning notice.</p><p>Write the class, the defect and what you did. The CP12 must still be issued. It protects you, and it puts the repair on the landlord.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "faq",
      "heading": "Questions, answered",
      "html": "<h3>What must a CP12 include by law?</h3><p>Under Regulation 36 of the 1998 Regulations: the date of the check, the property address and the landlord's name and address. Each appliance or flue, with its make, model, type and location. Any defects and the action taken. A note that each appliance is safe. Your name, Gas Safe number and signature, and the next check date.</p><h3>What happens if a landlord has no CP12?</h3><p>The HSE can serve an improvement notice or prosecute. Insurers may also refuse to pay after a gas incident.</p><h3>How long must a landlord keep a CP12?</h3><p>At least 2 years. Most solicitors say keep it for good.</p><h3>Can you issue a CP12 if an appliance is unsafe?</h3><p>Yes. You must. The record shows the finding, the class (Immediately Dangerous, At Risk or Not to Current Standards) and what you did.</p>"
    }
